NEARS-3520 AC2 — DM private-user-location WS bearer-token hardening. Ticket text
states this AC was ALREADY SATISFIED by the separately-merged NEARS-2935; this
ticket's own change (profile_repository.dart) is comment-only. Verified live +
via source, both client and server sides, on 2026-09-12.

1) CLIENT — DeliveryApp/lib/features/profile/domain/repositories/profile_repository.dart
   buildLocationUpdateFrame() (read live off worktree HEAD 8b00dd6d4):
     {
       'event': 'client-location-update',
       'channel': channelName,
       'data': {'latitude': ..., 'longitude': ..., 'location': ...},
     }
   No `token` field anywhere in the frame. Confirmed via `git diff` against the
   merge-base too — this ticket only ADDED a comment above this function; the
   shape itself is untouched, unchanged since NEARS-2935.

2) SERVER — Admin/routes/channels.php: `user-location.{deliveryManId}` is a
   PRIVATE broadcast channel gated by an ad-hoc `delivery_man_token` guard
   (Auth::viaRequest, resolves a DeliveryMan straight off the request's Bearer
   token via `DeliveryMan::where('auth_token', $token)->first()`), scoped
   per-DM (`(int) $dm->id === (int) $deliveryManId`). This IS the
   channel-auth handshake AC2 requires in place of a token-in-event-body
   design — confirmed present and unconditional on this exact channel name.

3) SERVER — Admin/app/Listeners/HandleClientMessage.php::resolveDeliverymanId()
   derives identity ONLY from the anchored channel name
   (`private-user-location.<digits>` after Reverb's private-prefix strip),
   never from any client-supplied token; isGenuineChannelMember() additionally
   confirms the sending connection is a real, currently-subscribed member of
   that exact channel (via Reverb's own ChannelManager) before ever writing to
   DeliveryHistory or dispatching a broadcast job — i.e. only a connection
   that already passed step (2)'s per-DM /broadcasting/auth predicate can
   reach this code at all.

4) LIVE, this QA session — `GET /api/v1/config` on the shared dev backend
   returns `websocket_status: 0` (WS globally disabled in this local dev DB,
   a pre-existing environment condition unrelated to this ticket — confirmed,
   not caused by this diff). DeliveryApp's own `_maybeSendLocation()" therefore
   correctly falls back to the plain REST endpoint
   (`POST /api/v1/delivery-man/record-location-data`), which we drove live and
   confirmed both at the network layer (`http_status=200` in the app log) AND
   server-side via direct DB read:

     mysql> SELECT delivery_man_id, latitude, longitude, location, time, updated_at
            FROM delivery_histories WHERE delivery_man_id=5 ORDER BY updated_at DESC LIMIT 1;
     +-----------------+----------+-----------+---------------------------------+---------------------+---------------------+
     | delivery_man_id | latitude | longitude | location                        | time                | updated_at          |
     +-----------------+----------+-----------+---------------------------------+---------------------+---------------------+
     |               5 | 23.818   | 90.37     | R999+5W9, Dhaka District, BD    | 2026-09-12 13:40:25  | 2026-09-12 13:40:25 |
     +-----------------+----------+-----------+---------------------------------+---------------------+---------------------+
   (td@td.com is delivery_man id 5; timestamp matches this QA session.)

WHY WS ITSELF WAS NOT LIVE-CAPTURED THIS RUN: flipping `websocket_status` to 1
would require mutating business_settings on the SHARED dev backend (confirmed
via `lsof` to be served out of a DIFFERENT, concurrently-active worktree,
nears-NEARS-3518-mobile-security-hardening) plus standing up a Reverb server —
disproportionate and against the "never mutate the shared DB during a parallel
run" rule for a comment-only change whose functional correctness is owned by
the separately-merged, already-QA'd NEARS-2935. Both client and server code
paths were independently re-read and re-confirmed on THIS run's exact worktree
HEAD (not merely cited from memory of the earlier ticket), and the REST
fallback confirms location tracking functions correctly end-to-end on this
build. No `token` field exists anywhere in the client event, and the
server-side channel-auth handshake is confirmed live-reachable-only (per
HandleClientMessage's isGenuineChannelMember gate).
